seg-f7007cf35f53 = IMU into the hybrid and fusion scenarios (<c1/>)

# text
# c1: hybrid
# c2: fusion
# c3: [accel_quantum]
# c4: [accel_classical]
# c5: bias
# c6: q_va
# c7: gyro_bias
# c8: q_arw
# c9: bias_instability
# c10: imu-fit
seg-3fc254763f67 = The <c1/> and <c2/> (17-state UKF) scenarios are simulations: they take sensor noise parameters (<c3/>, <c4/>: <c5/>, <c6/>, <c7/>, <c8/>, <c9/>), not a measured series. <c10/> reads those parameters off a stationary IMU log, so a scenario can be run with the sensor you actually have:

# text
# c1: allan::overlapping_adev_curve
# c2: q_va = (sigma(tau0) sqrt(tau0))^2
# c3: q_arw
# c4: bias
# c5: gyro_bias
# c6: bias_instability
# c7: q_aa
# c8: imu_fit
# c9: imu_fit
# c10: fit_imu_log
# c11: /kshana-fit-imu
seg-86efab73b2e6 = Method: the overlapping Allan deviation of the integrated signal (<c1/>): <c2/>, the same for the z gyro (<c3/>), <c4/> and <c5/> as the means, and <c6/> as the curve minimum when the curve turns up after it. <c7/> is not fitted (it needs a log far longer than the floor). The report carries the log's rate and span and the early Allan slope of each channel (about -0.5 where white noise dominates); a log that is not still, or is too short (under { "2000" } samples) or unevenly sampled, is refused or gives parameters the slopes flag. The table's provenance names the log's SHA-256. MODELLED: the estimator is checked on synthetic white-noise logs with known coefficients (recovered within { "10" } %), not against an external oracle. Available on the CLI, Python (<c8/>), WebAssembly (<c9/>), MCP (<c10/>) and the plugin (<c11/>).

# text
# c1: format = "rtcm3"
seg-b3c592f63798 = RTCM { "3" } MSM4 and MSM7 (<c1/>)

# text
seg-0f8b986cff40 = A byte stream of RTCM { "3" } frames (a base station's or a receiver's observation output). Read: MSM4 and MSM7 messages (GPS 1074/1077, GLONASS 1084/1087, Galileo 1094/1097, SBAS 1104/1107, QZSS 1114/1117, BeiDou 1124/1127, NavIC 1134/1137): the epoch, and the carrier-to-noise ratio of every signal cell (MSM4: { "6" } bits, dB-Hz; MSM7: { "10" } bits, 1/16 dB-Hz), which is what the C/N0 monitors need. Pseudorange and phase fields are stepped over, not used, so RTCM gives the trust monitors C/N0 and nothing else; there is no position in MSM. Frames with a bad CRC-24Q are skipped and counted.

# text
# c1: NOTICE
# c2: GPS TOW <s>
seg-d70038a737dc = <b>Where the layout comes from.</b> Not from the RTCM standard (a paid document, not consulted): from the open-source decoder pyrtcm { "1.2.0" }, BSD 3-Clause, whose licence and notice are in <c1/> (owner decision of { "18" } Oct { "2026" }: "use open decoders"). pyrtcm states that its tables follow RTCM { "10403.3" }; Kshana relies on the published tables under pyrtcm's licence and makes no claim about the standard. Time: MSM carries no week, so labels read <c2/>; GLONASS epochs (Moscow time) are moved to GPS time with GPS-UTC = { "18" } s (the value since 2017-01-01).

# text
# c1: 123456789
# c2: 0xCDE703
seg-ee04a86f8f00 = Checked against: the CRC-24Q catalogue check value (<c1/> gives <c2/>) and, as a one-off during development, pyrtcm's own decoder on the same self-written frames (same C/N0). The fixtures are self-written; no capture was taken from a real receiver, so the reader is MODELLED until it has read one.

